          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is cognitive behavioural therapy described as first-line for insomnia rather than medication?",
          "answer": "The sources I searched give three main reasons CBT for insomnia is placed ahead of medication.\n\n**Lasting effects.** The sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance (guideline-insomnia-cbt-first-line) says CBT for insomnia is first-line for chronic insomnia in adults. Its benefits continue after treatment ends, while the effects of medication generally do not.\n\n**Fewer long-term harms.** The same guidance notes that CBT avoids the harms linked to long-term sleeping-pill use. The general clinical reference on insomnia (condition-insomnia) adds that CBT responds durably to non-drug treatment, which makes long-term reliance on sleeping medicines avoidable for most people. Medication is described there as second-line and time-limited.\n\n**What the treatment involves.** The guideline describes CBT as a package of about four to eight sessions. It includes stimulus control, which means using the bed only for sleep and getting up when unable to sleep. It also includes sleep restriction, which limits time in bed toward actual sleep time.\n\nThe sources also say that sleep hygiene advice alone is not effective treatment for chronic insomnia. The lifestyle note (lifestyle-insomnia-sleep) says daily habits such as a consistent wake time support sleep but do not replace first-line treatment.\n\nThe search did not cover how CBT compares with medication in specific people, such as those with other health conditions, nor how to access CBT. A clinician can help with those questions. The sources also list signs that call for further assessment beyond insomnia treatment, including loud snoring with witnessed breathing pauses, excessive daytime sleepiness, falling asleep while driving, and sleep problems alongside low mood or hopelessness.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
